meta {
  name: List Service Tickets
  type: http
  seq: 10
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/crm/service-tickets?pageSize=5
  body: none
  auth: bearer
}

params:query {
  pageSize: 5
  ~page: 1
  ~tab: overdue
  ~sort: activity
  ~category: Renewal Review
  ~status: open,overdue
  ~search: smith
  ~archived: true
  ~scope: own
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{crmAccessToken}}
}

headers {
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("returns a paginated envelope, not a bare array", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.be.an("object");
    expect(body.items).to.be.an("array");
    expect(body.page).to.be.a("number");
    expect(body.pageSize).to.be.a("number");
    expect(body.total).to.be.a("number");
    expect(body.totalPages).to.be.a("number");
  });

  test("honours the requested page size", function () {
    // The cap is what makes this pagination rather than a suggestion: without
    // it a client could ask for the collection back and undo the change.
    expect(res.getBody().items.length).to.be.at.most(5);
  });

  test("carries the three tab counts", function () {
    const counts = res.getBody().counts;
    expect(counts.all).to.be.a("number");
    expect(counts.overdue).to.be.a("number");
    expect(counts.waiting).to.be.a("number");
  });

  test("counts describe the filtered set, not the page", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    // `counts.all` is every ticket matching scope + filters; `items` is one
    // page of them. Equal only when the whole set fits on one page.
    expect(body.counts.all).to.be.at.least(body.items.length);
    expect(body.total).to.be.at.least(body.items.length);
  });

  test("rows arrive in urgency order — overdue first", function () {
    // The server ranks by urgencyRank -> urgencyAt -> priorityRank -> _id, so
    // the client renders them as given. Checking the leading bucket is enough
    // to catch the sort being dropped or reversed, which is the failure that
    // looks like nothing at all.
    const overdue = res.getBody().items.map(function (t) {
      return t.status === "overdue";
    });
    const lastOverdue = overdue.lastIndexOf(true);
    const firstOther = overdue.indexOf(false);
    if (lastOverdue !== -1 && firstOther !== -1) {
      expect(lastOverdue).to.be.below(firstOther);
    }
  });

  test("every ticket carries the fields the queue renders", function () {
    for (const ticket of res.getBody().items) {
      expect(ticket).to.have.property("id");
      expect(ticket).to.have.property("ticketNumber");
      expect(ticket).to.have.property("clientName");
      expect(ticket).to.have.property("category");
      expect(ticket).to.have.property("status");
      expect(ticket).to.have.property("priority");
      expect(ticket).to.have.property("daysOpen");
      expect(ticket).to.have.property("isStatusLocked");
      expect(ticket.timeline).to.be.an("array");
    }
  });

  test("leadStatus is null on list responses", function () {
    for (const ticket of res.getBody().items) {
      expect(ticket.leadStatus).to.equal(null);
    }
  });

  test("every row names its assignee, for the Everyone view", function () {
    // PAC-109: the queue shows the branch's tickets, so a row that cannot say
    // whose it is leaves the reader no way to pick one up.
    for (const ticket of res.getBody().items) {
      expect(ticket).to.have.property("assignedRep");
      expect(ticket).to.have.property("assignedUserId");
    }
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /crm/service-tickets

  The ticket queue. Backs the **Priority Ticket Queue** on the Service Dashboard
  (`/crm/service`), the **Ticket Workspace** feed (`/crm/tickets`) and the
  **Archived Tickets** list.

  - **Module:** `crm_service`
  - **Permission:** `crm_service:read`
  - **Data scope:** `branch` sees the caller's branch and `agency` the whole
    agency — and since **PAC-109**, so does `own`. A service ticket is shared
    work, so an `own`-scoped CSR reads their whole branch rather than only the
    rows assigned to them, and the `?scope=` param is how they narrow back.
    This is the opposite of `GET /leads`, where `own` stays pinned.

    The widening lives in `ServiceTicketsService.scopeFilter`
    (`src/crm/ticket-scope.ts`) rather than on the CSR role template on
    purpose: `DataScope` is one value per user, read by every module, so
    moving the CSR to `agency` would open leads, deals and households at the
    same time.

  ## ⚠ Paginated since PAC-98 — this used to return `ServiceTicketView[]`

  It returned **every** ticket in the caller's scope in one response, and the
  browser did the ranking, the paging and the tab counts. That worked until a
  rep's queue reached several hundred, at which point the dashboard's first
  paint grew with the size of the book. The response is now an envelope and the
  work happens in Mongo.

  ## Params
  | Param | In | Notes |
  |---|---|---|
  | `page` | query | 1-based. Default 1. |
  | `pageSize` | query | Default 8, **capped at 100**. Past the cap is a 400, not a clamp — a silent clamp is how "pagination" becomes a suggestion. |
  | `tab` | query | `all` (default) · `overdue` · `waiting`. The queue's three tabs, as a server-side predicate. |
  | `sort` | query | `urgency` (default) · `activity`. The order *inside* each urgency band — overdue still leads, then workable, then blocked, then done, under both. `activity` puts the most recently touched ticket (`lastActivityAt`) first within its band. |
  | `category` | query | One of `SERVICE_TICKET_CATEGORIES`. |
  | `status` | query | One stored status, or several ORed — comma-separated or repeated (`open,overdue`). **Also switches off the active-queue default below**, so this is how the workspace's Resolved tab reaches resolved tickets. The Priority Ticket Queue sends every non-terminal status. |
  | `search` | query | Free text, tokenized (PAC-101): every word must match one of client name, ticket number, category or policy number. A phone-shaped term (7+ digits) also matches the stored phone in any format. Max 120 chars. |
  | `archived` | query | `true` returns only tickets past the archive window; omitted returns the active queue. |
  | `scope` | query | `own` \| `others` \| `agency`; clamped to the caller's scope (PAC-109). `own` = assigned to me. `others` = everyone else's, **unassigned included** — `own` and `others` partition the scope, which is what the dashboard's My Tickets / Agency Tickets parent tabs need. `agency` (the default) = everything my data scope reaches, mine included. None of them widen: a hand-edited value reaches no further than the caller's `DataScope` already did. |

  ## Response — `ServiceTicketListResponse`

  ```json
  {
    "page": 1,
    "pageSize": 8,
    "total": 577,
    "totalPages": 73,
    "items": [ /* ServiceTicketView */ ],
    "counts": { "all": 706, "overdue": 577, "waiting": 27 }
  }
  ```

  - **`total`** is the count for the *current tab*; `totalPages` derives from it.
  - **`counts`** is all three tabs over the filtered set (scope, category,
    search, archive window) but **before** the tab narrows it — which is what
    the tab chips have always meant: "how many would this tab show".

  ## Ordering — render as given

  Rows come back in urgency order: `urgencyRank` → `urgencyAt` → `priorityRank`
  → `_id`. That ranking is a total order across the whole queue, so **a client
  must not re-sort a page**: doing so orders that page against itself rather
  than against the pages either side of it.

  The first three are stored columns, materialized precisely so this sort can be
  served by an index — a computed sort key cannot be, and Mongo would answer
  with an in-memory sort over the caller's entire scope, which is the cost
  pagination exists to remove. `_id` is the final tiebreak because it is unique:
  a non-unique tiebreak lets a row appear on two pages or neither.

  See `migrations/20260910123159-queue-sort-indexes.js` for the two indexes that
  serve it (one per data scope) and the measurements behind them.

  ## The active queue excludes finished work (PAC-109)

  With no `status` and no `archived`, the list drops `resolved` and `closed`
  tickets — the queue is work still to do. It used to carry everything resolved
  inside the 7-day archive window, which is why the All tab read 277 beside an
  "Active Load" card of 254 on the same screen.

  Two escapes, each with a page behind it:

  - `?status=resolved` — the workspace feed's **Resolved** tab. An explicit
    status is the caller naming what they want, and it wins.
  - `?archived=true` — the **Archived Tickets** page, which asks for exactly
    the terminal side of the window.

  So the archive window no longer decides whether a resolved ticket clutters
  the queue; it decides which of those two views holds it.

  ## Statuses are stored, not derived

  A scheduled onboarding or renewal call takes its status from step timing, and
  that used to be computed on every read — leaving the stored column stale and
  the tab filters unusable server-side. `SyncTicketStatusFn` now advances the
  column on a five-minute cron, so `?tab=` and `?status=` mean what they say.
  The cost is bounded staleness: a call that crossed its deadline within the
  last tick may still read `open`.

  ## Errors
  | Status | When |
  |---|---|
  | 400 | `pageSize` above 100, unknown `tab`/`status`/`category`, `search` over 120 chars |
  | 401 | Missing/expired bearer token |
  | 403 | `crm_service` disabled for the agency, or the caller lacks `crm_service:read` |
}
